explanation:
Road Traffic Rules, clause 801. White signals on a black background in traffic lights intended for scheduled public transport have the following meanings:
801.1. an S-shaped signal prohibits the movement of scheduled public transport;
801.2. a horizontal bar signal prohibits the movement of scheduled public transport (except in the cases set out in clauses 29 and 166 of the Rules) and indicates that the traffic light signals are about to change;
801.3. an S-shaped signal and a horizontal bar signal switched on together prohibit the movement of scheduled public transport and indicate that an arrow-shaped signal (signals) will shortly be switched on;
801.4. an arrow-shaped signal (signals) permits the movement of scheduled public transport in the indicated direction (directions).

Drivers are advised to avoid unnecessary overtaking manoeuvres. Before starting to overtake, a driver must take additional precautions and make sure that:
136.1. none of the drivers following behind has started to overtake them;
136.2. the driver of the vehicle to be overtaken is not signalling a left turn;
136.3. the part of the lane needed for overtaking is clear and that oncoming vehicles, or pedestrians moving on the carriageway, will not be obstructed;
136.4. a safe distance from the overtaken vehicle will be kept during the overtake;
136.5. on finishing the overtake they will be able to return to their own lane without obstructing the overtaken vehicle.

Children under 135 cm tall who are carried in a car or a lorry must be restrained by child restraint systems suited to their height and weight, according to the size range and maximum child weight stated by the manufacturer, or according to these groups:
199.1. group 0 is for children weighing less than 10 kg;
199.2. group 0+ is for children weighing less than 13 kg;
199.3. group I is for children weighing between 9 and 18 kg;
199.4. group II is for children weighing between 15 and 25 kg;
199.5. group III is for children weighing between 22 and 36 kg.

Road Traffic Rules, clause 154. At a junction of roads of unequal priority, a driver travelling on the minor road must give way to vehicles approaching the junction on the major road.
Road Traffic Rules, clause 12. Road users must give way immediately to approaching emergency vehicles with blue and red (or only blue) beacons and sirens switched on, and to the vehicles they are escorting. Way is given to such vehicles and an emergency corridor is formed as follows:
12.1. on a road with one lane in each direction, drivers must stop on the right-hand shoulder or, where there is none, at the right-hand edge of the carriageway;
12.2. on a road with two or more lanes in one direction, drivers moving in that direction in the far left lane must move as far left as possible, right up to the corresponding edge of the carriageway used for that direction, and stop, while drivers moving in the same direction in the other lanes to the right of the far left lane must move as far right as possible, towards the right-hand edge of the carriageway or the shoulder, and stop;
12.3. on a road with a central reservation, the requirements of clauses 12.1 and 12.2 of the Rules must be followed only by drivers moving in the same direction.

Road Traffic Rules, clause 116. U-turns are prohibited:
116.1. at pedestrian crossings;
116.2. at level crossings;
116.3. in tunnels;
116.4. on and under bridges, flyovers and viaducts;
116.5. at places where the road is visible for less than 100 metres in at least one direction;
116.6. on roads with a central reservation, except at turning places built for that purpose and marked with road signs, or with road signs together with road markings.

Clause 173. A road user is prohibited from:
173.1. crossing the railway at places not intended for it;
173.2. passing other vehicles that have stopped before a level crossing to let a rail vehicle pass;
173.3. driving or walking onto a level crossing when the barrier is down or is starting to come down, raising the barrier without authorisation, or driving (or walking) around it;
173.4. driving onto a level crossing if there is an obstruction beyond it that would force the driver to stop on the crossing;
173.5. taking agricultural, road, construction and other machinery that has not been specially prepared for transport across a level crossing, if this could damage the equipment of the level crossing;
173.6. crossing a level crossing in a wheelchair without an accompanying person;
173.7. lingering or standing about on a level crossing, or taking longer than is needed to walk or drive across it.

Road Traffic Rules, clause 136. Drivers are advised to avoid unnecessary overtaking manoeuvres. Before starting to overtake, a driver must take additional precautions and make sure that:
136.1. none of the drivers following behind has started to overtake them;
136.2. the driver of the vehicle to be overtaken is not signalling a left turn;
136.3. the part of the lane needed for overtaking is clear and that oncoming vehicles, or pedestrians moving on the carriageway, will not be obstructed;
136.4. a safe distance from the overtaken vehicle will be kept during the overtake;
136.5. on finishing the overtake they will be able to return to their own lane without obstructing the overtaken vehicle.

Road Traffic Rules, clause 199. Children under 135 cm tall who are carried in a car or a lorry must be restrained by child restraint systems suited to their height and weight, according to the size range and maximum child weight stated by the manufacturer, or according to these groups:
199.1. group 0 is for children weighing less than 10 kg;

199.2. group 0+ is for children weighing less than 13 kg;

199.3. group I is for children weighing between 9 and 18 kg;

199.4. group II is for children weighing between 15 and 25 kg;

199.5. group III is for children weighing between 22 and 36 kg.
